partialText:
&lt;p&gt;Last week I wanted a single question answered: if a 30-day Chinese tourist visa was used on 31 December, what is the last legal day in the country? The obvious answer, "31 December", is wrong. The correct one is 30 January, and finding out why took four documents, one of which is a 2021 reply to a stranger's question on a government website.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is a write-up of the search, because the shape of it repeats: the statute gives a number, the regulation gives a definition, and the actual grading sits one layer further down, where no one looks.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Layer 1: the statute gives the outer penalty
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The Exit and Entry Administration Law (in force since July 2013) says this about overstaying, in &lt;a href="https://www.nia.gov.cn/n741440/n741547/c1013311/content.html" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;article 78&lt;/a&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;A foreigner who illegally resides shall be given a warning; where the circumstances are serious, a fine of 500 yuan per day of illegal residence, with the total not exceeding 10,000 yuan, or detention of not less than five days and not more than fifteen days.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Two things are missing from that sentence. It does not define what counts as a day of illegal residence, and it does not say when "the circumstances are serious". Both matter: the difference between a warning and detention is the difference between a stamp in a passport and a stay in a cell.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Layer 2: the regulation defines the clock, in the wrong place
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The State Council regulation underneath the law (&lt;a href="https://www.gov.cn/zhengce/content/202508/content_7036507.htm" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Order No. 637 of 2013, revised in 2025 by Order No. 814&lt;/a&gt;) contains the counting rule. It is not in the chapter on stay management. It is in article 36, the definitions article at the very end:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The stay period stated in the visa means the time the holder is permitted to stay after each entry, counted from the day after entry.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That one clause produced the 30 January answer. Entry on 31 December means day one is 1 January, and thirty days from there is 30 January. The arrival day is not a day of your stay. Nothing in the visa sticker says this.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The same article also settles a second trap. Article 13 gives the police counter a receipt "valid for no more than 7 days", which reads as a calendar week. The last paragraph of article 36 says every approval period and receipt period in the regulation is counted in working days, excluding public holidays. A seven-day receipt is therefore up to nine or ten calendar days around a holiday.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Layer 3: the tiers are in a Q&amp;amp;A reply
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Article 78 sets 500 yuan per day, capped at 10,000 yuan, for "serious" cases. The National Immigration Administration's &lt;a href="https://www.nia.gov.cn/Enquiry/publish/showQuestion.jsp?MZ=k0dulDWvsciKR0MEQHY4DA%3D%3D" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;published answer to a 2021 question&lt;/a&gt; fills in the tiers:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;10 days or less past the visa: warning.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;10 to 60 days past the visa: 500 yuan per day, &lt;strong&gt;counted from day 11&lt;/strong&gt;, capped at 10,000 yuan, or five days' detention.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;60 days or more, or a repeat overstay of 20 days or more: 10,000 yuan, or five to fifteen days' detention.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A visa-free overstay of up to 20 days: 500 yuan per day, with no grace period mentioned.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The "counted from day 11" parenthetical is doing a lot of work. It means a 12-day overstay costs 500 yuan, not 6,000. It also means the cap arrives at day 30, and every day from 31 to 60 is free at the margin, because the total cannot exceed 10,000 yuan.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I found no other official document that prints those brackets. The statute does not, the regulation does not, and the city-level service guides I read only reproduce the statute's sentence.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The code I used to check my own arithmetic
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Dates and money are where I make mistakes, so I wrote both calculations as assertions rather than trusting a mental count:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="k"&gt;from&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;datetime&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;import&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;timedelta&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;last_legal_day&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;stay_days&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# art. 36(3): the stay is counted from the day after entry&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;entry&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;timedelta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;days&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;stay_days&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;def&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;overstay_fine&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;days&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;visa_free&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;bool&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kc"&gt;False&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;tuple&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kt"&gt;int&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="kt"&gt;str&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"""Published grading: returns (fine, sanction)."""&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;days&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;and&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;not&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;visa_free&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"warning"&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;visa_free&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;days&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;20&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
            &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;days&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;500&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10_000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"fine or 5 days detention"&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10_000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"10,000 or 5-15 days detention"&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;days&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;&amp;lt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;60&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;
        &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;min&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;max&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;days&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;*&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;500&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10_000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"fine or 5 days detention"&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10_000&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"10,000 or 5-15 days detention"&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="k"&gt;assert&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;last_legal_day&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;2026&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;12&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;31&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;30&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;2027&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;30&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;assert&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;overstay_fine&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;10&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s2"&gt;"warning"&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;assert&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;overstay_fine&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;11&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;500&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;assert&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;overstay_fine&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;30&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10_000&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;assert&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;overstay_fine&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;45&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10_000&lt;/span&gt;          &lt;span class="c1"&gt;# cap, not 17,500&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;assert&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;overstay_fine&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;20&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;visa_free&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kc"&gt;True&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;0&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;==&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="mi"&gt;10_000&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The last two asserts are where the published text and intuition part company. In the visa band the fine is capped from day one, so 20 days hits the ceiling. In the visa-free band there is no ten-day grace written in, so the ceiling arrives earlier.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What the documents could not answer
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Three questions stayed open, and I left them open in the write-up rather than filling them with a plausible guess:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Applying late.&lt;/strong&gt; The law says apply at least seven days before the stay ends. It does not say what a counter does with an application filed with three days left.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Whether the ceilings are granted.&lt;/strong&gt; The service guide lists a maximum extension per visa category, 30 days for tourist visas and 180 for several business and family categories. Nothing published says how often the maximum is granted.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;How the grading lands in practice.&lt;/strong&gt; No public statistics on warnings, fines and detention exist, so the tiers describe a standard, not outcomes.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What I would tell the next person doing this
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Read the definitions article first. In Chinese administrative texts the definitions sit at the end of the regulation, and they carry the rules that change the arithmetic: what a day is, which days count, whether "7 days" means working days. The operative articles assume you have already read them.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Also check the revision date before citing an article number. The regulation was revised in 2025, and the 2025 amendment added one visa category and renumbered items. A blog post citing the 2013 numbering will look right and be wrong.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The full write-up, with the tables and the article-by-article citations, is at &lt;a href="https://notebookfield.com/posts/china-visa-stay-clock-and-overstay/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;notebookfield.com&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Drafted with an AI assistant from my own reading of the primary documents; every clause number and link here was checked against the source.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


  Adding a Structured Metadata Manifest to Content‑Automation for Reliable CI Publishing
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;TL;DR:&lt;/strong&gt; I introduced a &lt;code&gt;metadata.json&lt;/code&gt; file per content batch so the CI pipeline can discover, validate, and route posts without relying on fragile folder naming. This change makes the automation deterministic and ready for multilingual scaling.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  The Problem
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Our &lt;code&gt;content-automation&lt;/code&gt; repo drives a GitHub Actions workflow that reads markdown files, builds front‑matter, and pushes the payload to Dev.to, Bluesky, and a static site generator. The workflow used a &lt;strong&gt;directory‑scan&lt;/strong&gt; approach:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c"&gt;# pseudo‑code used in the old pipeline&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; file &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; content/**/post.md&lt;span class="o"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;do&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="c"&gt;# infer language from folder name, e.g. content/en/...&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="c"&gt;# infer date from folder hierarchy, e.g. content/2026/11/15/&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="k"&gt;done&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;When we added a new language (&lt;code&gt;fr&lt;/code&gt;) and started grouping posts by &lt;strong&gt;topic&lt;/strong&gt; instead of date, the scanner started missing files and, worse, mis‑routing posts to the wrong platform. The error we saw in the Action logs was:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight plaintext"&gt;&lt;code&gt;Error: Unable to determine language for ./content/2026/11/15/post.md
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The root cause was that the pipeline &lt;strong&gt;assumed&lt;/strong&gt; a fixed folder structure (&lt;code&gt;content/&amp;lt;year&amp;gt;/&amp;lt;month&amp;gt;/&amp;lt;day&amp;gt;/&amp;lt;lang&amp;gt;/post.md&lt;/code&gt;). Any deviation broke the discovery step, leading to silent drops and duplicated posts.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  What I Tried First
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;My first instinct was to &lt;strong&gt;normalize the folder names&lt;/strong&gt; on the fly:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// attempt 1 – normalizePath()&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;normalizePath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;parts&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;p&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;split&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'/'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// try to guess language by matching known codes&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;lang&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;parts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;find&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;part&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'en'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'es'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'de'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;includes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;part&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// fallback to default 'en'&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;lang&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;lang&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;||&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'en'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;date&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;parts&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;slice&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;1&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="mi"&gt;4&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'-'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;I added this helper to the loader script and ran the workflow locally. It worked for existing content, but when a post was placed directly under &lt;code&gt;content/2026/11/15/&lt;/code&gt; without a language subfolder, the function defaulted to &lt;code&gt;'en'&lt;/code&gt;. That caused &lt;strong&gt;incorrect language tags&lt;/strong&gt; on the published article, which we caught only after the post went live.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The approach also introduced a &lt;strong&gt;maintenance burden&lt;/strong&gt;: every time we added a new language or changed the folder hierarchy we had to update the heuristic list. It was clear we needed a more explicit contract between the content and the automation.&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;h2&gt;


  The Implementation
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  1. Define a JSON manifest per batch
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I created a new file &lt;code&gt;content/2026/11/15/content-automation/metadata.json&lt;/code&gt;. The diff from the commit looks like this:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight diff"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+{&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+  "repo": "content-automation",&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+  "date": "2026-11-15",&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+  "languages": [&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+    "es",&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+    "en"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+  ],&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+  "topics": [&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+    "Productivity"&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+  ],&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+  "commits": 4,&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+  "pull_requests": null&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="gi"&gt;+}&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The manifest captures everything the pipeline needs:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Field&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Why it matters&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;repo&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Allows the same script to be reused across multiple repos.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;date&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Single source of truth for the batch date (used for front‑matter).&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;languages&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Explicit list; no guessing.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;&lt;code&gt;topics&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Drives tag generation on each platform.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;
&lt;code&gt;commits&lt;/code&gt; / &lt;code&gt;pull_requests&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Optional metrics for analytics dashboards.&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  2. Update the loader (&lt;code&gt;src/loader.js&lt;/code&gt;)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I added a small module that reads the manifest, validates it, and returns a typed object. The file now lives at &lt;code&gt;src/loader.js&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// src/loader.js&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;require&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'fs'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;require&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'path'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="kd"&gt;function&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;loadMetadata&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;batchPath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;metaPath&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;batchPath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'metadata.json'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;existsSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;metaPath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`Metadata file missing at ${metaPath}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;raw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;readFileSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;metaPath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'utf-8'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;let&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;try&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="n"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;JSON&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;parse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;raw&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;catch&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;e&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`Invalid JSON in ${metaPath}: ${e.message}`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Minimal validation – keep it lightweight for CI&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;required&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'repo'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'date'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'languages'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'topics'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;of&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;required&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;key&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
      &lt;span class="k"&gt;throw&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;new&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;Error&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`Metadata validation error: missing "${key}"`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Export for the workflow script&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;module&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;exports&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;loadMetadata&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The CI script (&lt;code&gt;.github/workflows/publish.yml&lt;/code&gt;) now calls this loader:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight yaml"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;# .github/workflows/publish.yml (excerpt)
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;Load batch metadata&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;id&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;|&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s"&gt;node -e "const {loadMetadata}=require('./src/loader');&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s"&gt;const meta=loadMetadata('content/2026/11/15/content-automation');&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s"&gt;console.log(JSON.stringify(meta));&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="s2"&gt;" &amp;gt; /tmp/meta.json
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;-&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="na"&gt;name&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;Set output&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="na"&gt;run&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;:&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s"&gt;echo "metadata=$(cat /tmp/meta.json)" &amp;gt;&amp;gt; $GITHUB_OUTPUT&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


  3. Wire the manifest into the publishing logic
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In &lt;code&gt;src/publish.js&lt;/code&gt; I replaced the heuristic language detection with the explicit list from the manifest:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight javascript"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="c1"&gt;// src/publish.js (excerpt)&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;loadMetadata&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nb"&gt;require&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'./loader'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="nf"&gt;loadMetadata&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;env&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;BATCH_PATH&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

&lt;span class="n"&gt;meta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;languages&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;forEach&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;lang&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&amp;gt;&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;mdPath&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;path&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;join&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nb"&gt;process&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;env&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="no"&gt;BATCH_PATH&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`${lang}.md`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="o"&gt;!&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;existsSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;mdPath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;{&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="nb"&gt;console&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;warn&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="sb"&gt;`⚠️ No markdown for language "${lang}" – skipping`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
    &lt;span class="k"&gt;return&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;content&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;fs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;readFileSync&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;mdPath&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="s1"&gt;'utf-8'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;frontMatter&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="sb"&gt;`---
title: "My Post"
date: ${meta.date}
tags: ${meta.topics.join(', ')}
lang: ${lang}
---\n`&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// Build payload for each platform&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="kd"&gt;const&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;payload&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;frontMatter&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;content&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
  &lt;span class="c1"&gt;// sendPayload(payload, platform);&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;span class="p"&gt;}&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;h3&gt;


  4. Add a tiny schema test (future‑proofing)
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;While not required for the first release, I committed a&lt;/p&gt;




&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Part of my &lt;a href="https://dev.to/zaerohell"&gt;Build in Public&lt;/a&gt; series — sharing the real process of building SaaS projects from Playa del Carmen, México.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;Repo: &lt;code&gt;zaerohell/content-automation&lt;/code&gt; · 2026-11-16&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;#playadev #buildinpublic&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h1&gt;


  HyperDX API keys put observability access behind a team boundary
&lt;/h1&gt;

&lt;p&gt;HyperDX is an observability platform for logs, metrics, traces, and session replays. Its team settings hold API access keys, and the project's MCP documentation describes Bearer token authentication for an agent-facing endpoint. A HyperDX instance can therefore hold two kinds of sensitive authority: the telemetry it stores and the tokens that can query or manage that telemetry.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A count-only ZoomEye probe on 18 November 2026 used page 1, one result per page, and &lt;code&gt;sub_type=all&lt;/code&gt;. &lt;code&gt;app="HyperDX"&lt;/code&gt; returned 0, &lt;code&gt;title="HyperDX"&lt;/code&gt; returned 157, and &lt;code&gt;http.body="HyperDX"&lt;/code&gt; returned 656. These are field matches. They do not show how many production teams use the software, and they do not prove that an API key is exposed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Separate ingestion from management
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Telemetry ingestion and management access have different risk. An ingestion key that only writes logs is not equivalent to a personal key that can query dashboards, modify alerts, or use the MCP server. The HyperDX documentation distinguishes a personal API access key used for the external API and MCP from the credential used to send OTLP data. Inventory those credentials separately and record their scope, owner, creation time, expiry, and rotation path.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The project changelog also describes team API keys with descriptions and expiry and the ability to rotate personal access keys. Those features reduce the lifetime problem only when operators use them. A long-lived key generated once at account creation and never rotated remains a durable credential, even if the product now supports replacement.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The 157 title matches are the more focused control-plane population. The 656 body matches include documentation and discussions that mention HyperDX. A query that finds the brand in a page title does not tell whether the API is exposed, whether login is enabled, or whether the instance belongs to an organization.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Review the exposed routes
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Confirm the front-end host, API host, ingestion route, and any &lt;code&gt;/api/mcp&lt;/code&gt; endpoint. The MCP documentation says not every deployment exposes the backend directly, so use the deployment's actual proxy and route configuration. Put the management API and MCP endpoint behind the same identity and network policy as the team interface.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Review team membership and sign-in. If the instance uses local accounts, enforce individual identities and remove departed users. If it uses OIDC, verify the team and group mapping and test what happens when a user is removed. Check whether a browser session can be reused after removal and whether API keys survive account changes. Those are separate controls.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Audit key usage at the API and proxy layers. Alert on keys used from unexpected networks, queries over unusually long time ranges, new dashboard or alert changes, and calls to the MCP endpoint from hosts that are not registered automation systems. Do not put raw keys in dashboards, alerts, query labels, or support tickets.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Make the count useful
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Use ZoomEye to find branded pages outside the central inventory, then verify ownership with approved requests. Record the instance URL, team, authentication mode, API route, MCP exposure, and key owners. A title match without an owner is a lead. A managed service that the platform team already knows about is not a new finding.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Compare observations over time to catch a new deployment or a proxy change. A drop in title matches can reflect a login page with a generic title rather than a shutdown. The analyst should preserve the exact field query and recheck the host before changing the asset ledger.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;HyperDX is valuable because it brings operational evidence into one place. That concentration makes key scope and team boundaries part of the security review. The external count identifies candidate services. Key inventory and route checks determine whether the observability plane is controlled.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  References
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/hyperdxio/hyperdx/blob/main/MCP.md" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;HyperDX MCP authentication documentation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://github.com/hyperdxio/hyperdx" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;HyperDX project documentation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://www.zoomeye.ai/searchResult?q=dGl0bGU9Ikh5cGVyRFgi" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;ZoomEye title-field measurement&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;"The internet is down" covers six different problems, and only one of them deserves the word global. Deciding which one you're in is the first job of an incident, because the fix, the people you call, and the customer message all differ by class.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;a href="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F2oiuqazjvzcnvcder4eo.png" class="article-body-image-wrapper"&gt;&lt;img src="https://media2.dev.to/dynamic/image/width=800%2Cheight=%2Cfit=scale-down%2Cgravity=auto%2Cformat=auto/https%3A%2F%2Fdev-to-uploads.s3.us-east-2.amazonaws.com%2Fuploads%2Farticles%2F2oiuqazjvzcnvcder4eo.png" alt=" " width="799" height="449"&gt;&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Local ISP or customer-premises failure.&lt;/strong&gt; Your office fiber, a modem, an ONT, a regional ISP footprint. Everything is unreachable from one location while the same services work from a phone on cellular. Most common class by a wide margin.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Regional carrier or backbone disruption.&lt;/strong&gt; A transit provider, subsea cable or metro ring loses capacity. Multiple independent networks in one geography lose the same paths.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;DNS or BGP incident.&lt;/strong&gt; A bad route announcement or a resolver failure makes healthy services unreachable for millions of users with no intrusion anywhere.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Cloud or SaaS dependency outage.&lt;/strong&gt; One provider's region, AZ or control plane fails and every product built on it degrades at once.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;DDoS or confirmed cyberattack.&lt;/strong&gt; Resource exhaustion by traffic. An availability attack, which is a different thing from an intrusion that compromises systems or data.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Coincident reports that only look global.&lt;/strong&gt; Several unrelated incidents in the same hour, user-report maps light up, social media supplies the word "global."
That last class is underrated and it's usually the answer. Thousands of providers run incidents every day; on any given afternoon several overlap. A check across nine provider status pages on 17 November turned up exactly that shape — Cloudflare with R2 errors from South America and some AI models, Apple with iCloud sign-in problems, Twilio with eight carrier-specific incidents across seven countries, OpenAI with a delayed Costs API. Microsoft, GitHub and Google Cloud reported nothing; AWS showed two long-running regional issues in UAE and Bahrain.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every one bounded by product, region or carrier. None attributed to malicious activity. A genuine global outage needs a &lt;em&gt;shared&lt;/em&gt; layer to fail: a backbone, a major DNS or BGP path, or a cloud control plane the others depend on.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The diagnosis matrix
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Work it top to bottom. Starting at "have we been hacked" and working backwards burns the first hour chasing the rarest explanation.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Symptom&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Likely layer&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;First check (&amp;lt;5 min)&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What confirms it&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What isn't enough&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Unreachable from one office; cellular works&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Local ISP / CPE&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Modem or ONT lights; ISP status page; a second site on the same ISP&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;ISP notice naming your footprint&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A spike on a national outage map&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Several sites in one region lose the same destinations&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Regional carrier&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Traceroute from two providers in the region&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Multiple independent networks losing the same paths; route telemetry&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A list of unrelated SaaS apps being slow&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Sites "won't load" but IPs ping; SERVFAIL or NXDOMAIN&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;DNS&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Query a second resolver; test authoritative servers for your domains&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Resolver or authoritative failures plus a provider acknowledgement&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A user saying "the website is down"&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Specific destinations unreachable from many networks; paths change abruptly&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;BGP / routing&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Looking-glass or route-collector check for your prefixes and theirs&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Abnormal announcements or withdrawals plus an operator statement&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Social posts speculating about a hack&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;One vendor's products degrade together; your infra is healthy&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Cloud / SaaS&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Provider status page and your tenant's service-health view&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Provider incident naming region, service, impact&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Downdetector alone&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Edge floods with traffic; latency climbs&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;DDoS&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Edge and WAF dashboards; provider DDoS notice&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Traffic evidence plus provider or security confirmation and a mitigation record&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Outage size or suddenness&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Unexpected admin activity or config changes alongside the outage&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Intrusion&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Identity logs, change audit, EDR; open a security incident&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Forensic evidence of compromised accounts, or a provider disclosure&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Any loss of availability by itself&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Outage and security incident are different words
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;An outage is loss of availability. A security incident is evidence that confidentiality, integrity or control was compromised.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Outage&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;Security incident&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;What failed&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Reachability or capacity&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Trust: an account, system, dataset or control plane&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Who declares it&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Provider status page, NOC, your SRE lead&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Your security team, a provider disclosure, or a regulator&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Evidence&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Status incident, route telemetry, resolver errors&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Forensic artifacts, compromised credentials, attacker activity in logs&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;What ends it&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Service restored, confirmed by synthetic checks&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Containment, eradication, recovery, written root cause&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Customer message&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;"Provider X is degraded; here's the workaround and next update"&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Legal and compliance review before any statement&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A DDoS sits in both columns — an attack by intent, an outage by effect. And a vulnerability is a weakness, not an incident; it becomes one when someone exploits it. Treat an unpatched edge device as a reason to investigate during an outage, not as a reason to announce an attack.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The rule: use the word "attack" only after a responsible primary source confirms it. Everything before that is an outage of unknown cause, and that phrase is fine to put in a customer notice.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  First 15 minutes, first hour, first four hours
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;0–15 min — is this us, our ISP, or a provider?&lt;/strong&gt; Confirm the symptom from a second network. Check your ISP's status page and your own synthetic monitors. Identify one known-good path for the most critical flow. Open a channel and name an incident lead. The output is a scope statement with the evidence behind it, not a cause.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;15–60 min — which layer, and what do we tell customers?&lt;/strong&gt; Run the matrix. Pull the dependency map and list every customer-facing flow touching the affected layer. Publish one message with known scope, workaround, and next update time. Route everything else through the incident lead.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;1–4 hours — which fallback, and what do we preserve?&lt;/strong&gt; Activate only fallbacks tested in the last quarter. An untested failover at hour two turns one outage into two. Preserve logs, route snapshots, DNS query logs and edge captures with timestamps, because if the classification later moves toward attack, the logs that would settle it have usually rotated away.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Day 1–2.&lt;/strong&gt; Rebuild the timeline from evidence rather than memory. Review which flows failed, which fallbacks worked, which didn't exist.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  How much redundancy is worth buying
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Enough for the flows whose failure costs more than the redundancy. This runs against the commercial interest of anyone selling resilience work, so it's worth saying plainly.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Where independence pays&lt;/strong&gt;: a second ISP on a different physical path at a site that can't run on cellular; secondary DNS at a different provider with the zone actually replicated and tested; a provider-independent path for the one or two flows whose outage starts a regulatory clock or costs measurable revenue within the hour.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Where it costs more than it saves&lt;/strong&gt;: two clouds for a workload that tolerates four hours down; dual-vendor SaaS for email or chat; any failover you can't test quarterly. Every added provider is also added surface — a second identity integration, a second credential set, a second patch cycle.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Five decisions cover most of it: map dependencies before buying anything; define a known-good path per critical flow and who may switch to it; delete untested failovers from the runbook until they're exercised; retain evidence long enough to classify an incident days later; and decide in advance which status sources your incident lead trusts, so nobody classifies an incident from a social feed.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;A team that gets those right has most of the resilience it will ever use. A team that buys a second cloud before doing the dependency map has bought a second set of problems.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Full write-up — the November 17 provider-by-provider check, the full matrix, and the industry dependency chains: &lt;a href="https://teamvoy.com/blog/global-internet-outage/" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;teamvoy.com/blog/global-internet-outage&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Written by the Teamvoy team. More engineering writing at &lt;a href="https://teamvoy.com/blog" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;teamvoy.com/blog&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;When a rewrite changes "I cannot take on the extra 6 reports this month" into "I can take on the extra 6 reports this month," every name, number, and date survives. The checker sees all anchors present. The refusal has become a commitment.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That gap between literal token retention and semantic fidelity is the whole reason this fixture exists.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The fixture
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Download &lt;code&gt;cases.json&lt;/code&gt; from &lt;a href="https://hemmingway.io/downloads/email-rewrite-fidelity/cases.json" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;hemmingway.io/downloads/email-rewrite-fidelity/cases.json&lt;/a&gt;. It contains six synthetic English emails, each with:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;id&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;source&lt;/code&gt; (the original message)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;anchors&lt;/code&gt; (named literal strings that must survive)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;manual_invariants&lt;/code&gt; (conditions, negations, actor assignments, and tentativeness a reviewer must verify by hand)&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;These are not customer emails. No model has been run against them. They exist to teach the checking method.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  One concrete case: refusal and alternative
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Take &lt;code&gt;refusal_and_alternative&lt;/code&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Hi Sam, I cannot take on the extra 6 reports this month. I can review 2 outlines next month, but I have not agreed to write them. Would that help? Thanks, Arjun.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Anchors: &lt;code&gt;Sam&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;6&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;2&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;Arjun&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here is the deliberately corrupted offline checker control, not an AI output:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Hi Sam, I can take on the extra 6 reports this month. I can review 2 outlines next month, but I have agreed to write them. Would that help? Thanks, Arjun.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Construction: &lt;code&gt;source.replace('cannot take','can take').replace('have not agreed','have agreed')&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Every anchor survives. The checker returns &lt;code&gt;needs_manual_semantic_review&lt;/code&gt;. But two obligations flipped:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The refusal ("cannot take on") became a commitment ("can take on").&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The non-agreement ("have not agreed") became an agreement ("have agreed").&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This is a refusal/negation/commitment inversion, not a reversal of shipment conditionality. The literal tokens are identical except for the negation words.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The checker
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;code&gt;check_fidelity.py&lt;/code&gt; is a Python standard-library script. It reads an output JSON file (a flat object mapping each case ID to the rewritten string) and checks whether every anchor appears as a whole token in the normalized output text.&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight python"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;missing&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;=&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;anchor&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;for&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;anchor&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;in&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="k"&gt;case&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;[&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'anchors'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
           &lt;span class="k"&gt;if&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="ow"&gt;not&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;re&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;search&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'(?&amp;lt;!\w)'&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;re&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;escape&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="nf"&gt;normalized&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;(&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="n"&gt;anchor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="o"&gt;+&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;r&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="s1"&gt;'(?!\w)'&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;,&lt;/span&gt; &lt;span class="n"&gt;text&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span class="p"&gt;]&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;Statuses:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;missing_output&lt;/code&gt; — the output field is empty or absent.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;literal_check_failed&lt;/code&gt; — one or more anchors are absent.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;code&gt;needs_manual_semantic_review&lt;/code&gt; — all anchors present. &lt;strong&gt;This is not a pass.&lt;/strong&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Exit code: &lt;code&gt;0&lt;/code&gt; means every case reached &lt;code&gt;needs_manual_semantic_review&lt;/code&gt;. Exit &lt;code&gt;1&lt;/code&gt; means at least one case failed a literal check or has no output.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Running it
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Save all three files in one directory first:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;cases.json&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;check_fidelity.py&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;code&gt;outputs.example.json&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Then:&lt;br&gt;
&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="highlight js-code-highlight"&gt;
&lt;pre class="highlight shell"&gt;&lt;code&gt;cp outputs.example.json my_outputs.json
&lt;span class="c"&gt;# edit my_outputs.json with your rewritten strings&lt;/span&gt;

python3 check_fidelity.py my_outputs.json
&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;

&lt;/div&gt;



&lt;p&gt;The checker prints a JSON object with a &lt;code&gt;notice&lt;/code&gt; field and per-case results. The notice reads: "Literal survival is not semantic correctness. No automatic model pass is assigned."&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  The tested semantic control
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The exact tested control JSON is available separately at &lt;a href="https://hemmingway.io/downloads/email-rewrite-fidelity/tested-semantic-control.json" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;tested-semantic-control.json&lt;/a&gt;. It is not one of the six source cases, not a model-generated rewrite, and not an added benchmark result. It documents the &lt;code&gt;refusal_and_alternative&lt;/code&gt; inversion shown above, with construction, observed status, and the explicit note that &lt;code&gt;model_generated&lt;/code&gt; is &lt;code&gt;false&lt;/code&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Why human review is still required
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The five control checks validate the checker's logic, not any model. No model request has been sent for this dataset. No accuracy percentage, ranking, or zero-error claim can be derived from these fixtures.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;What the manual rubric catches:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Conditions flipped to commitments&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Negation dropped ("cannot" becomes "can"; "not agreed" becomes "agreed")&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Actor changes&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Certainty inflation&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Attachment claims&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Adapting the fixture
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The structure is portable. Replace the six sources with your own authorized synthetic or nonconfidential messages. Keep the same JSON shape: &lt;code&gt;id&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;source&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;anchors&lt;/code&gt;, &lt;code&gt;manual_invariants&lt;/code&gt;. The checker will work unchanged. Freeze your inputs and settings before running any evaluation. Preserve every output, including failures. Do not cherry-pick the best rewrites for a reported metric.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Limitations
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Six English synthetic cases. Not representative of all real email behavior.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Literal checks are not semantic correctness.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;No model benchmark has run. No consumer tool was tested.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;No automatic sending is involved.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;No legal or security guarantee.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

