  Technical resources
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://hemmingway.io/downloads/email-rewrite-fidelity/cases.json" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;cases.json&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://hemmingway.io/downloads/email-rewrite-fidelity/check_fidelity.py" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;check_fidelity.py&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://hemmingway.io/downloads/email-rewrite-fidelity/outputs.example.json" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;outputs.example.json&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;&lt;a href="https://hemmingway.io/downloads/email-rewrite-fidelity/tested-semantic-control.json" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;tested-semantic-control.json&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  Disclosures
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The publisher is Hemmingway, a writing-model company. This article was drafted by Hemmingway-1 on Medium with editorial review and local checker validation. No independent research or peer review was performed. The editorial drafting process is distinct from API or consumer model evaluation; nothing in this article tests the consumer rewriter or any model's output.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;This is a submission for the &lt;a href="https://dev.to/mlh-hackathon"&gt;MLH x DEV Writing Challenge&lt;/a&gt;.&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;How many tabs did you open the last time you prepared for a conference?&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Now picture a business angel preparing for Slush. One calendar. No analyst. 2,989 attendees on Cala's list. Five tabs per startup: website, LinkedIn, a funding database, search, news.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;That's about 15,000 tabs.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;And after all that, you get to the only part that matters, the decision, already tired.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;We spent 48 hours at Junction 2026 making that number zero. We finished &lt;strong&gt;third in Cala's track, Make Unicorns Real&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;This post covers three things:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ol&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;What we built&lt;/strong&gt;, and the one rule that keeps it honest.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;The trick that took us from 5 to 27 trusted startups&lt;/strong&gt; without adding a single data source.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;What breaks&lt;/strong&gt; when you build a real-time product on a young graph API, and how we worked around it.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ol&gt;

&lt;h2&gt;


  What I Built
&lt;/h2&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;First point: the product.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Grabbit&lt;/strong&gt; is a proactive research agent for investors who decide alone.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You give it a vague thesis: &lt;em&gt;"AI startups at pre-seed and seed."&lt;/em&gt;
&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It asks two or three questions, each backed by numbers from the candidate set.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You name the investors you already trust.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;It walks their funding rounds in Cala's graph, keeps the startups attending Slush, and ranks them by how close they sit to the people you respect.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;You correct the report in plain words, &lt;em&gt;"shorter, one line per startup"&lt;/em&gt;, and it rewrites itself.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;When you leave, it keeps watching. If a startup on your shortlist moves, you get an alert.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Grabbit never tells you what to buy. The investor decides.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Here's the whole idea seen from the investor's side:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div class="table-wrapper-paragraph"&gt;&lt;table&gt;
&lt;thead&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What the investor wants&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What usually gets in the way&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;th&gt;What Grabbit does&lt;/th&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/thead&gt;
&lt;tbody&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A shortlist worth their time&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Too many candidates (2,989)&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Ranks by proximity to investors they already trust&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;To believe the numbers&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Data with no clear source&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A source badge on every fact, from Cala's &lt;code&gt;source_rank&lt;/code&gt;
&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;To get there fast&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;Hours of manual research&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;A first report from a single chat turn, then alerts&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;tr&gt;
&lt;td&gt;To do less work&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;~15,000 tabs&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td&gt;No tabs. You talk to the report&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/tbody&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Second point: where it came from.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;It didn't start with a stack. It started with two investor interviews, kept anonymous in our research notes. One of them summed it up:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;em&gt;"I have no time to scout; if a tool pre-selects and I review each pick by hand, I've already won."&lt;/em&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Junction's Cala challenge, &lt;a href="https://junction2026.com/challenges/cala-make-unicorns-real" rel="noopener noreferrer"&gt;Make Unicorns Real&lt;/a&gt;, asked for a product that solves a real need for founders or investors, powered by reliable public data. Slush started right after the hackathon. The brief said to aim for something they could start using the day after. That deadline became our constraint.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Third point: the team.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;I brought the first idea, ran the interviews, sat in the Cala briefings, coordinated the team, built the web app and cut the pitch film. I didn't do it alone:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;José Javier Bravo Sarabia&lt;/strong&gt; built the discovery engine (the reverse walk, the second ring of co-investors, the proximity score) plus the shared cache, the PWA and the deploy.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Juan López Bravo&lt;/strong&gt; owned the product flow and research docs, the Cala client and cache, report polish, the guided demo and the peer-review script.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;Rubén Moyano Palazón&lt;/strong&gt; shaped the agent's questions and focus, added live voice transcription, and ran the engine review and data audit.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Four people, each driving AI coding agents on separate branches. More than 100 reviewed pull requests. One weekend.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;h3&gt;


  The two lessons I'm keeping
&lt;/h3&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Lesson 1: if the question lives in a graph, don't read the big set in list order.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The naive way: go through Slush attendee by attendee. Cost: about &lt;strong&gt;9,000 Cala calls&lt;/strong&gt;, and you still miss most of the interesting companies.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The way that worked: start from the investors you trust, walk their rounds, &lt;em&gt;then&lt;/em&gt; intersect with the event.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The result on our real cache, with Y Combinator and 500 Global as the trusted pair:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Startups with a trusted investor in their rounds: &lt;strong&gt;5 → 27&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;
&lt;strong&gt;8 of the top 10&lt;/strong&gt; in our ranking were invisible before.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Reproducible from the repo.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Same data. Different direction. Five times the signal.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Lesson 2: an agent that invents a number is worse than no agent.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;So the model never touches the numbers:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Ranking is &lt;strong&gt;deterministic&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;Gemini writes &lt;strong&gt;only prose with placeholders&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;The server fills every value from a sourced fact.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;li&gt;A missing field reads &lt;strong&gt;"Not published"&lt;/strong&gt;, never zero.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Trust has to be visible on the fact itself, not in a footnote at the end.&lt;/p&gt;

